2.2 Control and metadata layer (the config-driven core)
The control database makes the pipeline config-driven: onboarding a new table means adding a row, not writing new code.
CREATE TABLE ctl.table_register (
  source_table      VARCHAR,
  target_db         VARCHAR,
  target_schema     VARCHAR,
  target_table      VARCHAR,
  pk_columns        VARCHAR,     -- 'store_id,item_id,date'
  load_type         VARCHAR,     -- FULL | CDC
  cdc_filter        VARCHAR,     -- e.g. 'sales_date >= current_date - 7' (limit CDC scope)
  cse_required      BOOLEAN,     -- skip encryption for non-sensitive data
  is_active         BOOLEAN
);

CREATE TABLE ctl.event_log (
  message_id        VARCHAR,
  source_table      VARCHAR,
  version_current   INT,
  version_previous  INT,
  status            VARCHAR,     -- NEW | EXTRACTING | STAGED | LOADED | MERGED | FAILED
  received_ts       TIMESTAMP,
  processed_ts      TIMESTAMP
);
The watcher (step 2): A Python job on a schedule reads the event bus, saves every message to event_log, then keeps only the tables listed in table_register. Messages are saved first because the bus carries events for every table from every tenant, and saving them makes reprocessing and audit possible.
2.3 CDC extract (Spark)
from pyspark.sql import functions as F

def cdc(prev_df, curr_df, pk_cols, filter_expr=None):
    if filter_expr:                       # limit CDC scope on huge transactional tables
        prev_df, curr_df = prev_df.where(filter_expr), curr_df.where(filter_expr)

    assert dict(prev_df.dtypes) == dict(curr_df.dtypes), "Schema drift detected"

    non_pk = [c for c in curr_df.columns if c not in pk_cols]
    p, c = prev_df.alias("p"), curr_df.alias("c")
    j = p.join(c, on=pk_cols, how="full_outer")

    changed = F.lit(False)
    for col in non_pk:
        changed = changed | ~F.col(f"p.{col}").eqNullSafe(F.col(f"c.{col}"))

    flag = (F.when(F.col(f"p.{non_pk[0]}").isNull() & F.col(f"c.{non_pk[0]}").isNotNull(), "I")  # simplify w/ a presence marker in real code
             .when(F.col(f"c.{non_pk[0]}").isNull() & F.col(f"p.{non_pk[0]}").isNotNull(), "D")
             .when(changed, "U").otherwise("N"))
    ...
    # keep I/U/D, take 'c.*' values for I/U and 'p.*' for D, add CDC_FLAG + BATCH_ID + EXTRACT_TS
In production, either use the G-Research df1.diff(df2, *pk_cols) function or add an _exists marker column to each side so inserts and deletes are detected reliably, including rows where values are null.
Delta time travel gives you both versions: spark.read.format("delta").option("versionAsOf", v).
Full extract: curr_df.withColumn("CDC_FLAG", F.lit("I")).
File sizing: use repartition(n) so each file is roughly 10–100 MB compressed, the range Snowflake loads most efficiently.
Metadata file: write a JSON file next to the data with columns, types and primary keys. Later steps use it for schema validation and to create tables automatically.

Simpler option on newer platforms: if the source has Delta Change Data Feed enabled (readChangeFeed), you can skip the version-to-version comparison. The HLD's comparison approach works no matter how the source tenant loads its tables, which matters in a multi-tenant platform.
2.4 Client-side encryption (envelope pattern)
Create a key-encryption key (KEK) (AES-256) in Key Vault or KMS and set an expiry date (the Coles standard requires rotation at least every two years).
For each file, generate a random content-encryption key (CEK), encrypt the file with it, then wrap the CEK with the KEK. Snowflake expects its own CSE format, so follow Snowflake's client-side encryption spec and the cloud SDK.
Encryption has to be a separate step after the Parquet files are written. Spark can't encrypt while it writes a DataFrame (call-out J in the HLD), so you need an extra persistence step.
For non-sensitive tables, cse_required = false lets the pipeline skip encryption and copy the files directly.

