Before running `COPY INTO`, compare the metadata file with `INFORMATION_SCHEMA.COLUMNS`. If the table doesn't exist yet, create it from the metadata and apply the RBAC grants. That step is optional; most tables are created in advance through CI/CD.

### 2.6 Dynamic MERGE (apply CDC)

```python
def build_merge(tgt, src, pks, cols):
    on   = " AND ".join(f"t.{k}=s.{k}" for k in pks)
    upd  = ", ".join(f"t.{c}=s.{c}" for c in cols if c not in pks)
    ins_c = ", ".join(cols); ins_v = ", ".join(f"s.{c}" for c in cols)
    return f"""
    MERGE INTO {tgt} t
    USING (SELECT * FROM {src}
           QUALIFY ROW_NUMBER() OVER (PARTITION BY {','.join(pks)} ORDER BY EXTRACT_TS DESC)=1) s
    ON {on}
    WHEN MATCHED AND s.CDC_FLAG='D' THEN UPDATE SET t.IS_DELETED=TRUE, t.UPDATED_TS=CURRENT_TIMESTAMP
    WHEN MATCHED AND s.CDC_FLAG IN ('U','C','I') THEN UPDATE SET {upd}, t.IS_DELETED=FALSE, t.UPDATED_TS=CURRENT_TIMESTAMP
    WHEN NOT MATCHED AND s.CDC_FLAG <> 'D' THEN INSERT ({ins_c}, IS_DELETED, UPDATED_TS)
         VALUES ({ins_v}, FALSE, CURRENT_TIMESTAMP);"""
Deletes set a logical delete flag instead of removing rows, matching the HLD's Persisted Staging design.
Removing duplicates with QUALIFY makes the merge idempotent, so replaying a batch gives the same result.

