3. How to explain it in an interview
30-second version
"I worked on a data ingestion pipeline that replicated Delta Lake tables from our Databricks-based enterprise data platform into Snowflake for BI. It was event-driven and config-driven. When a table was published, we ran a Spark CDC job comparing the current and previous table versions, wrote Parquet delta files, encrypted them client-side with AES-256 envelope encryption using Key Vault, staged them in ADLS, loaded them through an encrypted Snowflake stage with COPY INTO, and applied the changes with a dynamically generated MERGE into a persisted staging layer. Onboarding a new table was a config row, not new code."
2-minute structure (STAR)
Situation: Snowflake had been approved for BI, but there was no approved, secure way to move data from the lakehouse into it.
Task: Design a reusable pipeline that supports full and incremental loads and composite keys, meets data-protection standards, and is cheap to run.
Action: Walk through the 7 steps (event → watcher → orchestrator → CDC → encrypt → COPY INTO → MERGE). Then mention the two security decisions: Tri-Secret Secure was assessed and rejected, and client-side encryption was adopted.
Result: One standard pattern approved by architecture and security, new tables onboarded through metadata, and three alternative patterns documented for other latency or cost needs.

Only claim the parts you actually built. If your role was implementing or supporting the pipeline rather than designing it, say so. Interviewers usually probe deeply.
Design trade-offs to bring up
Why a version-to-version comparison instead of the source's own change tracking? It works no matter how each tenant loads their tables, and gives every consumer the same I/U/D format. The cost is extra compute, which we reduced by filtering the comparison window on large transactional tables.
Why files instead of writing directly through the connector? Security and audit (data encrypted at rest, files available for replay), Snowflake loads files in bulk efficiently, and it works for sources outside the platform. The cost is more moving parts.
Why client-side encryption but not Tri-Secret Secure? The risk was the staging area in our own storage account. Snowflake already encrypts its internal storage, so adding a customer-managed key there added key-management overhead for little benefit.
Why save events to a database first? The bus carried events for every tenant. Saving them gave filtering, idempotency, retries and an audit trail.
Idempotency: dedupe by primary key and latest extract time, a status machine per batch, and MERGE so replays are safe.

Likely follow-up questions and short answers
How do you handle schema changes? Compare the metadata file with Snowflake's information schema before loading, and fail fast or evolve through a controlled CI/CD change.
How do you handle late or duplicate events? Process versions in order per table using the event log, and dedupe inside the MERGE.
How do you scale to hundreds of tables? The master pipeline launches parallel runs per table, Parquet files are 10–100 MB, and loading warehouses are sized by measured baselines.
What happens when the encryption key rotates? Drain the staged files first, then create the new key, recreate the Snowflake stage, and re-encrypt any files kept for replay.
How would you make it real-time? Pattern #2 (Snowpipe triggered by storage events), or Snowpipe Streaming or Kafka, plus a scheduled or stream-and-task MERGE.
What would you change today? Use Delta Change Data Feed instead of comparing whole versions, Snowflake dynamic tables or streams and tasks for the MERGE layer, possibly Iceberg tables to avoid copying data, and private connectivity everywhere.

